{"judgments": [
  {"clause_id": "c001",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The execution date is left illegible/blank (\"July KX, 2005\"), yet the whole running of the covenant depends on it because Section 1(D) measures the Restricted Period from \"the date of this Agreement.\" A court could be asked to fix when the three-year period began and ended.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.24}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c002",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "Recital A is an ungrammatical fragment: the \"Pursuant to the terms and conditions of the Stock Purchase Agreement ... among Buyer and Sellers\" clause has no verb, and the operative statement of what Buyer is purchasing is severed into a separate sentence. The date of the Stock Purchase Agreement is also left blank, so the recital does not identify the instrument it incorporates by date.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.15}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c003",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "This recital locates the consideration for the covenant in Buyer's willingness to close the Stock Purchase Agreement, while the operative terms make the Company (not Buyer) the promisee, Buyer being only a third-party beneficiary under Section 5(A). A court could be asked whether the recital supplies the consideration and the sale-of-business character that makes the Section 2 covenant enforceable by the Company.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.26}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c004",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "This recital imports definitions from an unattached Stock Purchase Agreement, and Section 1(C) then uses \"Acquired Companies,\" a term defined nowhere in this instrument. Whether the covenant's key defined terms can be ascertained at all turns on reading this recital together with Section 1(C).", "type": 2, "prob": 0.22}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c005",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The closing qualifier \"of health insurance claims for hospitals and physicians in the United States\" is ambiguous: it may modify only the data-conversion items in the list, or the entire enumerated set, and \"in the United States\" may describe where the customers are or where the services are performed. The scope of \"Business\" — and thus of the covenant — turns on which reading applies.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.38},
     {"issue": "This definition of \"Business\" is narrower and differently worded than Section 1.2 of the contemporaneous Employment Agreement, which covers activities \"in the United States and India\" and is expressly non-exhaustive (\"including but not limited to\"). A court must decide which definition governs conduct restricted by both instruments.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.28}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c006",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The definition is circular and self-referential: \"Company\" is defined to include any entity that is a \"successor ... subsidiary or affiliate of the Company,\" using the term being defined, and \"Ajuba\" itself is never a defined term. The class of protected entities therefore has no ascertainable boundary.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.25},
     {"issue": "The preamble defines \"Company\" as Ajuba International, Inc. alone, while this section expands the same defined term to subsidiaries, affiliates and future successors. A court could be asked which meaning \"the Company\" carries in the operative covenants and in Section 5(G).", "type": 2, "prob": 0.20}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c007",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The \"material contact\" limiter and the twelve-month look-back attach only to clause (c), so clause (a) sweeps in every client of Buyer or the Acquired Companies with no temporal limit and no requirement that the Shareholder ever dealt with them. The reach of the non-solicitation covenant depends on whether that limiter is read across the whole definition.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.28},
     {"issue": "\"Acquired Companies\" is used here but defined only in the unattached Stock Purchase Agreement via Background C, so the class of protected customers cannot be identified from this instrument. A court would have to read the two documents together to give the definition content.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.20}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c008",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "This three-year period runs from the date of this Agreement, whereas Section 1.14 of the contemporaneous Employment Agreement defines \"Restricted Period\" as three years after the end of the Employment Term — potentially six years. A court must decide which period governs the Shareholder's post-employment competition.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.38}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c009",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The Restricted Area here is the United States, its territories and protectorates, while Section 1.13 of the contemporaneous Employment Agreement defines the same term to include India, where the Shareholder resides and worked. A court would have to decide whether Indian activity is restricted, and which instrument's geographic scope controls.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.33}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c010",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "\"Engage in any activity which is in competition with the Business\" in any capacity \"or otherwise\" has no stated boundary, and the catch-all \"or otherwise\" follows an enumerated list of capacities (officer, director, employee, consultant, agent) inviting an ejusdem generis argument. The scope of prohibited conduct — and its reasonableness — is what a court would construe.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.45},
     {"issue": "These covenants overlap but do not match Article V of the contemporaneous Employment Agreement, which contains its own non-compete, customer non-solicitation and employee non-solicitation with different carve-outs (e.g. the Supervisor-and-above limitation and the \"Core Business\" substitution in Article V(d)). A court must decide how the two sets of restrictions operate together against the same person.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.35}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c011",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The section prescribes two inconsistent consequences for an invalid restriction — that it \"shall be deemed deleted herefrom\" and, in the proviso, that it \"instead shall be deemed limited\" — without stating which a court is to apply. The Shareholder's acknowledgment that the restrictions are \"reasonable in scope and duration\" also sits against the same section's assumption that a court may find them unenforceable.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.27}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c012",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "\"The foregoing covenant\" and \"the foregoing covenant not to compete\" are ambiguous as to whether the equitable-relief right reaches all of Section 2, including the customer and employee non-solicitation subsections, or only the Section 2(A)(1) non-competition promise. The remedy's reach turns on which reading applies.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.27}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c013",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "\"Confidential Information\" is defined by an open-ended \"shall include ... including, but not limited to\" list qualified only by \"not generally known in the business community,\" a phrase with no stated benchmark. The obligation is also perpetual (\"at any time, either during or subsequent to the Restricted Period\"), so its outer limit is fixed only by that vague qualifier.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.34},
     {"issue": "This definition and its carve-outs differ from Sections 1.5 and 3.1 of the contemporaneous Employment Agreement, which uses a different enumerated definition and permits disclosure \"to the extent disclosure is required by law\" without the advance-notice condition imposed here. A court would have to decide which confidentiality regime governs the same information.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.20}
   ]}
]}

